{#
    The catch-all error page, and deliberately the only template in this project that
    extends nothing and depends on nothing.

    A 500 is very often *caused* by the thing an error page would normally rely on.
    The database is unreachable, the container failed to compile, an environment
    variable is missing. If this template extended base.html.twig it would pull in the
    asset manifest, the route generator and app.user — and any one of those failing
    while rendering the error produces a blank white page instead of a message. The
    user then sees nothing at all, which is the one outcome worse than a 500.

    So: no extends, no path(), no asset(), no app.user, and the styling is inlined.
    A literal "/" is the only link, because it is the one URL that cannot be wrong.

    Symfony serves this only when APP_DEBUG is off. In dev it is previewable at
    /_error/500.
#}
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
    <title>Something went wrong — extdir</title>
    <meta name="robots" content="noindex">
    <style>
        :root {
            color-scheme: light dark;
            --bg: #fbfaf9; --fg: #1b1917; --muted: #605a52; --accent: #9a3f12; --border: #e3ded8;
        }
        @media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) {
            :root { --bg: #161513; --fg: #f2efea; --muted: #ada69b; --accent: #e8895a; --border: #322f2b; }
        }
        body {
            margin: 0; min-height: 100vh;
            display: grid; place-items: center;
            background: var(--bg); color: var(--fg);
            font-family: ui-sans-serif, system-ui, -apple-system, 'Segoe UI', sans-serif;
            line-height: 1.6; padding: 1.5rem;
        }
        main { max-width: 34rem; text-align: center; }
        .code {
            font-family: ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, Menlo, monospace;
            font-size: 2.5rem; color: var(--accent); margin: 0 0 .5rem; letter-spacing: -.04em;
        }
        h1 { font-size: 1.375rem; margin: 0 0 .75rem; letter-spacing: -.015em; }
        p { color: var(--muted); margin: 0 0 1rem; }
        a {
            display: inline-block; margin-top: .5rem;
            color: var(--fg); text-decoration: none;
            border: 1px solid var(--border); border-radius: 8px;
            padding: .6875rem 1rem; font-weight: 600;
        }
        a:hover { border-color: var(--accent); color: var(--accent); }
    </style>
</head>
<body>
<main>
    <p class="code">{{ status_code }}</p>
    {# Anything below 500 is a refusal or a bad request, not a fault. Claiming "this
       has been logged and someone will look at it" for a 403 or a 429 invites a bug
       report for an incident that never happened, and tells the visitor the one
       thing that is certainly untrue: that it was not about them. #}
    {% if status_code >= 500 %}
        <h1>Something went wrong at our end</h1>
        <p>
            This is a fault on the server, not anything you did. It has been logged and someone
            will look at it.
        </p>
        <p>Nothing you submitted was lost, and no data was affected.</p>
    {% else %}
        <h1>That request was refused</h1>
        <p>
            The address may need a permission you do not have, or the request was not one this
            page accepts.
        </p>
    {% endif %}
    <a href="/">Back to the directory</a>
</main>
</body>
</html>
